<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Höxter</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Höxter is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It grew up beside the abbey of Corvey, founded nearby in 822, whose abbots claimed lordship over it, and it was a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. Corvey's Carolingian westwork is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hoxter.jpg" alt="Höxter" width="320" height="270" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Spunky · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rathaus_H%C3%B6xter.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Höxter lies on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>, 52 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, in the heart of the Weser Uplands, and is the seat of the Höxter district. The district has about 30,000 people, with the town itself making up around half. Its older names include Hoxer and Huxaria. The municipality was formed on 1 January 1970 by merging eleven communities of the former Amt of Höxter-Land with the main town and Bruchhausen. One of them, Lüchtringen, is the easternmost community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, Höxter was a villa regia and the scene of a battle between his forces and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. Under the protection of the Princely Abbey of Corvey it gradually became prosperous and the chief town of the principality of Corvey. It lay on the long-distance trade route known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellweg">Hellweg</a>. Later it asserted its independence and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. Rivalry with Corvey Abbey and the nearby town called Corvey grew, and in 1265 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> of Höxter allied themselves with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a>, whose troops destroyed the town of Corvey and damaged the abbey.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> hit Höxter severely. In 1634 Imperial troops besieged the town in what became known as the Blutbad von Höxter, the massacre of Höxter. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 it was united with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>. In 1802 it passed to Nassau, in 1807 to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, and in 1814, after that kingdom was dismembered, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. In 2005 an explosion in a house in the historic centre killed three people and damaged the town hall and many other significant buildings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Höxter has a medieval town hall and a half-timbered old town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser-renaissance">Weser Renaissance</a> style, with historic houses with high gables and carved façades of the 15th and 16th centuries. The Protestant church of St Kilian has a pulpit of 1595 and a font of 1631, and a stone bridge about 150 metres long, built in 1833, crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>. Schloss Corvey, formerly Corvey Abbey, stands on the outskirts. Its abbey church has a Carolingian crypt and an imposing westwork, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-von-fallersleben">Hoffmann von Fallersleben</a>, author of Das Lied der Deutschen, worked there as a librarian and is buried next to the church.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Höxter” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Corvey was founded in 822 on the Weser near <b>Höxter</b> by monks from Corbie in Picardy, led by Adalard and Wala, cousins of Charlemagne, with the support of Louis the Pious.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-abbey-of-corvey">Prince-Abbey of Corvey</a></li></ul></section>
